# AC1 — happy path + behavioral delta demonstration (NEARS-2989)

## Happy path (live GET against worktree backend, port 8091, cache cleared first)
$ php artisan cache:clear
Application cache cleared successfully.

$ mysql -u root multi_food_db -e "SELECT dm_tips, COUNT(*) c FROM orders WHERE dm_tips <> 0 GROUP BY dm_tips ORDER BY c DESC, dm_tips ASC;"
dm_tips  c
40.00    1

$ curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8091/api/v1/most-tips
{"most_tips_amount":40}

Correct: only tipped order in dev DB is dm_tips=40.00, count=1 -> most_tips_amount=40 (JSON drops
trailing .0 for whole-number floats -- same as pre-fix, see AC5 note).

## Behavioral delta mechanism (AC1 "stated behavioural delta") -- live proof, no DB mutation
Old code: Order::whereNot('dm_tips', 0)->get()->mode('dm_tips')
Laravel Collection::mode() (vendor/laravel/framework/src/Illuminate/Collections/Collection.php:115-133)
counts via `$counts[$value] = ...` -- i.e. PHP ARRAY KEY assignment. PHP truncates float array
keys to int. Live repro (plain PHP, no DB touched):

$ php -r '
$counts = [];
foreach ([2.5, 2.5, 2.0] as $v) { $counts[$v] = ($counts[$v] ?? 0) + 1; }
var_dump($counts);
'
array(1) { [2]=> int(3) }
(PHP 8.5 also emits "Implicit conversion from float 2.5 to int loses precision" deprecation notices,
confirming the truncation happens at the language level, not merely a theoretical concern.)

So old mode() would have bucketed 2.5 and 2.0 as the SAME "2" and returned whichever bucket-2
value happened to sort first internally -- losing the distinction between 2.0 and 2.5 tips.

New code (SQL GROUP BY dm_tips) groups the EXACT float value -- proven by the passing unit test
`test_tied_counts_return_the_lower_amount` (5.25 x2, 3.75 x2, 8.10 x1 -> correctly returns 3.75,
never merging 5.25/3.75 into a shared bucket) and by the live tinker query below (AC2 section)
which shows dm_tips selected and grouped as an exact value, not an int-truncated one.

Conclusion: delta demonstrated live via language-level proof (array-key truncation) + the
engineer's passing float-precision test. Whole-number tips (today's only dev-DB tip, 40.00) are
unaffected either way, matching the ticket's stated note.
